CertHub.register({"id":"cka","vendor":"The Linux Foundation / CNCF","name":"Certified Kubernetes Administrator","short":"CKA","exam":"CKA (Kubernetes v1.35 curriculum)","blurb":"Hands-on Kubernetes exam for administrators who install, configure, upgrade, network, store data on and troubleshoot production-grade clusters.","status":"verified","statusNote":"Domains and weights read on Sept 24, 2026 in the CNCF CKA Curriculum v1.35 PDF (github.com/cncf/curriculum): Storage 10%, Workloads and Scheduling 15%, Services and Networking 20% (printed as \"Servicing and Networking\"), Cluster Architecture, Installation and Configuration 25%, Troubleshooting 30%. The Linux Foundation training site could not be opened from here, so exam length (2 hours, performance-based) and the 66% pass mark come from the Linux Foundation FAQ as quoted in search results; confirm them on the exam page before booking. The exam tracks the current Kubernetes minor release, so check the curriculum version again if you book months from now.","lastVerified":"2026-09-24","notices":[],"examInfo":{"questions":"About 15–20 performance-based tasks on live Kubernetes clusters","minutes":120,"pass":"66%","extra":"The real exam has no multiple-choice questions: you SSH to nodes and solve tasks with kubectl, kubeadm, etcdctl, crictl, systemctl, Helm and YAML, switching between several clusters, with the Kubernetes docs open. The practice exam on this site is a 60-question, 90-minute knowledge check of the same objectives; pair it with timed hands-on practice on kubeadm VMs, kind or minikube (and the killer.sh simulator sessions included with registration)."},"examSim":{"questions":60,"minutes":90},"sources":[{"label":"CNCF CKA Curriculum v1.35 (PDF, GitHub)","url":"https://github.com/cncf/curriculum/blob/master/CKA_Curriculum_v1.35.pdf"},{"label":"Linux Foundation CKA exam page","url":"https://training.linuxfoundation.org/certification/certified-kubernetes-administrator-cka/"},{"label":"Linux Foundation CKA/CKAD/CKS FAQ","url":"https://docs.linuxfoundation.org/tc-docs/certification/faq-cka-ckad-cks"}],"planWeeks":10,"hoursPerWeek":"6–8","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Cluster Architecture, Installation and Configuration","w":25,"topics":["Control plane and node components: kube-apiserver, etcd, kube-scheduler, kube-controller-manager, kubelet, kube-proxy, container runtime","Preparing hosts for kubeadm: containerd, matching systemd cgroup driver, swap, overlay and br_netfilter modules, ip_forward and bridge sysctls, required ports","kubeadm init (pod network CIDR, control-plane endpoint), installing a CNI plugin, kubeadm join and bootstrap tokens","Static Pods and /etc/kubernetes/manifests; kubeadm certificates (check-expiration, renew) and kubeconfig files","Cluster upgrades with kubeadm: one minor version at a time, upgrade plan/apply/node, drain, kubelet upgrade, uncordon, version skew","etcd backup and restore with etcdctl/etcdutl and the etcd PKI; pointing the etcd static Pod at a restored data directory","Highly available control planes: stacked vs external etcd, quorum, load balancer in front of API servers, --upload-certs and --certificate-key","RBAC: Roles, ClusterRoles, RoleBindings, ClusterRoleBindings, aggregated and built-in roles, users via CertificateSigningRequests, kubectl auth can-i","Node maintenance: cordon, drain, uncordon and PodDisruptionBudgets","Helm (repo add/update, upgrade --install, --version, values) and Kustomize (kubectl apply -k) for installing cluster components","Extension interfaces: CRI (containerd, CRI-O, crictl), CNI (Calico, Cilium, Flannel), CSI drivers","CustomResourceDefinitions, custom resources and operators (CRD plus controller)"],"notes":["Curriculum v1.35: Manage RBAC; prepare infrastructure; create and manage clusters with kubeadm; manage the cluster lifecycle; implement a highly-available control plane; use Helm and Kustomize to install cluster components; understand extension interfaces (CNI, CSI, CRI); understand CRDs, install and configure operators"],"labs":["On two or three local VMs (VirtualBox, UTM or Multipass), install containerd with SystemdCgroup = true, load overlay and br_netfilter, set the sysctls, then kubeadm init with --pod-network-cidr, install Calico or Flannel and join a worker.","On that cluster, back up etcd with `etcdctl snapshot save` (with the /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd flags), delete a namespace, restore with `etcdutl snapshot restore` to a new data dir and edit the etcd static Pod manifest to bring the namespace back.","Upgrade the cluster one minor version with kubeadm (plan, apply, drain, kubelet upgrade, uncordon), then create a user with a CSR, bind her to a Role in one namespace and prove it with `kubectl auth can-i --as`."]},{"id":2,"name":"Workloads and Scheduling","w":15,"topics":["Deployments and ReplicaSets as self-healing primitives; rolling updates, rollout status, history, undo and restart","DaemonSets and StatefulSets from an operator's point of view, including tolerations for control plane nodes","ConfigMaps and Secrets: creation, env and volume consumption, immutable objects, restarting workloads after changes","Resource requests and limits, LimitRange and ResourceQuota as admission controls","Scheduling: nodeSelector, nodeName, node affinity (required vs preferred)","Taints and tolerations: NoSchedule, PreferNoSchedule, NoExecute and tolerationSeconds","Pod affinity and anti-affinity with topologyKey; topologySpreadConstraints","PriorityClasses and preemption","Workload autoscaling: HorizontalPodAutoscaler (kubectl autoscale, autoscaling/v2), metrics-server and CPU requests; awareness of VPA and Cluster Autoscaler","Static Pods vs scheduler-placed Pods; what happens when the scheduler is down"],"notes":["Curriculum v1.35: Understand deployments, rolling updates and rollbacks; use ConfigMaps and Secrets; configure workload autoscaling; understand the primitives for robust, self-healing deployments; configure Pod admission and scheduling (limits, node affinity, etc.)"],"labs":["In a three-node kind cluster, taint one worker dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule, label it, and deploy one Pod that lands only there (toleration plus node affinity) and a Deployment that avoids it.","Install metrics-server in kind or minikube (`minikube addons enable metrics-server`), create a Deployment with CPU requests, `kubectl autoscale` it and drive load with a busybox loop until the HPA scales out.","Create two PriorityClasses, fill a small minikube node with low-priority Pods, then submit a high-priority Pod and watch the preemption events with `kubectl get events -w`."]},{"id":3,"name":"Services and Networking","w":20,"topics":["Kubernetes network model: one IP per Pod, NAT-free Pod-to-Pod traffic, the CNI plugin's role, Pod and Service CIDRs","kube-proxy modes (iptables, IPVS, nftables) and how Service virtual IPs work","Service types: ClusterIP, NodePort (30000–32767), LoadBalancer (cloud controller or MetalLB), headless and ExternalName","EndpointSlices, Services without selectors, externalTrafficPolicy and sessionAffinity","NetworkPolicies: default deny, ingress and egress rules, podSelector, namespaceSelector (kubernetes.io/metadata.name), ipBlock, ports","Gateway API: GatewayClass, Gateway listeners and allowedRoutes, HTTPRoute parentRefs, matches and weighted backendRefs; installing the CRDs and a controller","Ingress controllers, IngressClass and the default class; Ingress rules, path types and TLS","CoreDNS: the coredns ConfigMap and Corefile, forwarding and stub zones, Service and Pod DNS records, dnsPolicy","Testing connectivity with temporary Pods (busybox, nicolaka/netshoot), nslookup, curl and nc"],"notes":["Curriculum v1.35: Understand connectivity between Pods; define and enforce Network Policies; use ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer service types and endpoints; use the Gateway API to manage Ingress traffic; know how to use Ingress controllers and Ingress resources; understand and use CoreDNS"],"labs":["Create a kind cluster with the default CNI disabled, install Calico, then apply a default-deny NetworkPolicy in a namespace and open only frontend-to-api on TCP 8080 plus DNS egress; test each rule with a busybox Pod.","Install the Gateway API CRDs and a free implementation (for example NGINX Gateway Fabric or Envoy Gateway) in kind, create a Gateway and an HTTPRoute that splits traffic 90/10 between two Deployments, and measure the split with a curl loop.","Edit the coredns ConfigMap in minikube to forward a test zone to a public resolver, watch CoreDNS reload, and check both cluster and external names with `kubectl run dnstest --rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 -- nslookup`."]},{"id":4,"name":"Storage","w":10,"topics":["Volumes vs PersistentVolumes; PV and PVC lifecycle (Available, Bound, Released) and binding rules","Access modes: ReadWriteOnce, ReadOnlyMany, ReadWriteMany, ReadWriteOncePod","Reclaim policies: Retain and Delete (Recycle deprecated) and cleaning up Released PVs","StorageClasses, provisioners and dynamic provisioning; the default StorageClass annotation","volumeBindingMode Immediate vs WaitForFirstConsumer","Volume expansion with allowVolumeExpansion","Static PVs: hostPath for labs, local volumes with nodeAffinity, NFS","CSI drivers and StatefulSet volumeClaimTemplates with PVC retention"],"notes":["Curriculum v1.35: Implement storage classes and dynamic volume provisioning; configure volume types, access modes and reclaim policies; manage persistent volumes and persistent volume claims"],"labs":["In kind or minikube, create a hostPath PV with Retain, bind it with a PVC, write data from a Pod, delete the PVC and bring the Released PV back to Available by clearing its claimRef.","Install the local-path provisioner (kind already ships it), create a StorageClass with WaitForFirstConsumer and make it the default, and watch a PVC stay Pending until a Pod uses it.","Deploy a three-replica StatefulSet with volumeClaimTemplates, scale it to one and back to three, and confirm the PVCs and their data were kept."]},{"id":5,"name":"Troubleshooting","w":30,"topics":["Node problems: NotReady, kubelet status and journalctl -u kubelet, kubelet config and certificates, container runtime down, node conditions (DiskPressure, MemoryPressure)","Control plane problems: static Pod manifests, crictl ps/logs when the API server is down, scheduler and controller-manager symptoms, etcd health with etcdctl","Pending Pods: FailedScheduling messages for resources, taints, affinity and unbound PVCs","Resource monitoring: metrics-server, kubectl top nodes/pods --sort-by, describe node allocated resources","Container output streams: kubectl logs options, stdout/stderr vs log files, /var/log/pods and /var/log/containers, sidecar log streaming","Events: kubectl get events with field selectors and sorting","Service and networking problems: selectors, readiness and EndpointSlices, kube-proxy, cross-node CNI traffic, sandbox network errors","DNS problems: CoreDNS Pods and logs, loop detection with systemd-resolved, testing with a temporary Pod","Cluster access problems: kubeconfig contexts, expired certificates, connection refused on 6443","Output handling for tasks: -o jsonpath, custom-columns, --sort-by, writing answers to files","Stuck Terminating Pods on lost nodes and force deletion"],"notes":["Curriculum v1.35: Troubleshoot clusters and nodes; troubleshoot cluster components; monitor cluster and application resource usage; manage and evaluate container output streams; troubleshoot services and networking"],"labs":["Break-fix drill on your kubeadm VMs: stop kubelet, typo the kube-apiserver manifest, and stop containerd one at a time; for each, find the cause using only journalctl, crictl and the manifests, then fix it.","In kind, install metrics-server with --kubelet-insecure-tls, then use `kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu`, `kubectl describe node` and a jsonpath query to write the busiest Pod and every node's InternalIP to files.","Break a Service three ways in minikube (wrong selector, wrong targetPort, failing readiness probe), then diagnose each with `kubectl get endpointslices`, describe and a temporary busybox Pod."]}],"study":{"1":[["Describe the order of a kubeadm upgrade from 1.34 to 1.35.","On the first control plane node: upgrade the kubeadm package, run kubeadm upgrade plan, then kubeadm upgrade apply v1.35.x. Drain the node, upgrade kubelet and kubectl, restart kubelet and uncordon. Repeat on other control plane nodes and workers with kubeadm upgrade node instead of apply. Never skip a minor version and never let kubelet get ahead of the API server."],["How do you back up and restore etcd on a kubeadm cluster?","Backup: etcdctl --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert, --cert and --key from /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd, then snapshot save <file>. Restore: etcdutl snapshot restore <file> --data-dir <new dir>, then change the etcd static Pod's data hostPath to the new directory and let kubelet restart etcd (and the API server)."],["What does a highly available kubeadm control plane need?","A load balancer (or VIP) in front of all API servers used as --control-plane-endpoint, at least three control plane nodes for etcd quorum (stacked) or a separate three-member etcd cluster (external), and extra control plane nodes joined with --control-plane --certificate-key after kubeadm init --upload-certs."],["When do you use a Role, a ClusterRole, a RoleBinding and a ClusterRoleBinding?","Role: rules for namespaced resources in one namespace. ClusterRole: cluster-scoped resources (nodes, PVs) or reusable rule sets. RoleBinding: grants a Role or a ClusterRole inside one namespace. ClusterRoleBinding: grants a ClusterRole in every namespace and for cluster-scoped resources."],["How do CRI, CNI and CSI fit together when a Pod with a PVC starts?","The CSI driver provisions and attaches/mounts the volume; kubelet asks the CRI runtime (containerd) to create the Pod sandbox; the runtime calls the CNI plugin to give the sandbox an IP and routes; then the containers start with the volume mounted."],["What is the difference between a CRD and an operator?","A CRD adds a new resource type to the API so objects can be stored and listed. An operator is a controller (usually a Deployment) that watches those objects and creates or manages the real resources. Without the controller nothing happens."]],"2":[["Explain taints and tolerations versus node affinity.","Taints repel: a node with a taint rejects Pods that do not tolerate it. Tolerations only lift that barrier. Node affinity (or nodeSelector) attracts Pods to labeled nodes. To dedicate nodes you usually combine a taint, a label, a toleration and an affinity rule."],["What do NoSchedule, PreferNoSchedule and NoExecute do?","NoSchedule blocks new Pods without a toleration. PreferNoSchedule is a soft version the scheduler tries to honour. NoExecute also evicts running Pods that do not tolerate it, optionally after tolerationSeconds."],["Why might an HPA not scale?","The Metrics API is missing (no metrics-server), containers lack CPU requests so utilization shows <unknown>, the target is already at maxReplicas, or the metric is below the target. kubectl describe hpa shows the conditions and events."],["How do you spread replicas across nodes or zones?","Required Pod anti-affinity with topologyKey kubernetes.io/hostname gives at most one per node. topologySpreadConstraints with maxSkew and topologyKey topology.kubernetes.io/zone keeps counts balanced, either strictly (DoNotSchedule) or as a preference (ScheduleAnyway)."],["What makes a Deployment self-healing?","The Deployment controller manages ReplicaSets, and the ReplicaSet controller keeps the number of matching Pods equal to replicas, replacing Pods that are deleted or lost with their node. Probes let kubelet restart unhealthy containers and remove unready Pods from Services."]],"3":[["How does traffic to a ClusterIP reach a Pod?","CoreDNS resolves the Service name to the ClusterIP. On the client's node, kube-proxy's iptables, IPVS or nftables rules translate the ClusterIP and port to one ready endpoint from the EndpointSlices, and the CNI network carries the packet to that Pod."],["Compare Ingress and the Gateway API.","Ingress is one resource for HTTP(S) routing with controller-specific annotations for anything advanced. The Gateway API splits roles: GatewayClass (implementation), Gateway (listeners, owned by the platform team) and HTTPRoute and other routes (owned by app teams), with built-in features like header matching and weighted backends. Both need a controller."],["Write the parts of a default-deny ingress NetworkPolicy.","apiVersion networking.k8s.io/v1, kind NetworkPolicy, spec.podSelector: {} (all Pods in the namespace), policyTypes: [Ingress] and no ingress rules. Add separate allow policies after it; a CNI with policy support is required."],["How do you change how the cluster resolves external names?","Edit the coredns ConfigMap in kube-system: change the forward line for the default upstream, or add a server block for a specific domain that forwards to a chosen DNS server. CoreDNS reloads the Corefile automatically."],["When do you use each Service type?","ClusterIP for in-cluster access; NodePort to expose on every node's IP at a port from 30000–32767; LoadBalancer for an external address from a cloud controller or MetalLB; headless (clusterIP: None) for per-Pod DNS records; ExternalName for a DNS alias to an outside name."]],"4":[["Walk through dynamic provisioning.","A PVC names a StorageClass (or gets the default). The class's provisioner (a CSI driver) creates a volume and a PV matching the request, which is bound to the PVC. With WaitForFirstConsumer this waits until a Pod using the PVC is scheduled, so the volume lands where the Pod can use it."],["What decides whether a PVC binds to an existing PV?","Same storageClassName (including an empty class), compatible access modes, capacity at least as large as the request, matching selector if the PVC has one, and the PV being Available (not Released with an old claimRef)."],["Compare Retain and Delete reclaim policies.","Delete removes the PV and the backing storage when the PVC is deleted (default for dynamic provisioning). Retain keeps both; the PV becomes Released and an admin must recover or delete the data and clear the claimRef before reuse."],["What is the difference between ReadWriteOnce and ReadWriteOncePod?","ReadWriteOnce allows read-write mounting by a single node, so several Pods on that node can share it. ReadWriteOncePod (CSI only) allows exactly one Pod in the cluster to use the volume."]],"5":[["A node is NotReady. What is your checklist?","kubectl describe node for conditions and messages; SSH in; systemctl status kubelet and journalctl -u kubelet; check the container runtime (systemctl status containerd, crictl ps); check certificates, the kubelet config path, disk space and whether a CNI plugin is installed and running."],["The API server is down. How do you troubleshoot without kubectl?","On the control plane node: crictl ps -a to see the kube-apiserver container state, crictl logs for its errors, journalctl -u kubelet for manifest parse errors, check /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml for typos and check etcd health with etcdctl endpoint health."],["A Service is not reachable. What do you check in order?","Service selector vs Pod labels; EndpointSlices for ready endpoints; Pod readiness probes; port and targetPort vs containerPort; DNS resolution from a test Pod; kube-proxy on the client node; NetworkPolicies; the CNI for cross-node traffic."],["How do you find the resource-hungry Pods and nodes?","kubectl top nodes and kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu or memory (needs metrics-server) for live usage, and kubectl describe node (Allocated resources) for what requests have reserved."],["Where do container logs live and how do you read them?","The runtime writes stdout and stderr to files under /var/log/pods on the node (linked from /var/log/containers). Read them with kubectl logs (-c, --previous, -f, --since, -l) or, when the API server is down, crictl logs or the files directly."],["Why can CoreDNS crash-loop on Ubuntu nodes and how do you fix it?","systemd-resolved puts 127.0.0.53 in /etc/resolv.conf; CoreDNS forwards to it and loops back to itself, which the loop plugin detects. Set kubelet's resolvConf to /run/systemd/resolve/resolv.conf (or forward to a real upstream) and restart the CoreDNS Pods."]]},"qCount":151,"hasLessons":true,"hasLessonsEs":true,"hasPbqs":true,"hasLevels":true});
